From 0c4a6b7163d95f350a0a3d34520cb57dc117fc82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 21:56:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drivers: add fpctee, FPC match-on-chip sensors behind a QSEE
 trustlet

Some Qualcomm phones -- the Google Pixel 2 XL is the one this was written
on -- wire an FPC match-on-chip sensor to an SPI bus TrustZone owns.
Enrolment, matching and template storage all happen inside the vendor's
trustlet; the kernel's fpc1020 driver provides /dev/fpc_tee to reach it,
report the sensor's interrupt level, and hand over the trustlet's file
requests.

The command protocol and its sequences follow the vendor's own HAL: the
capture loop of fpc_tee_capture_image(), the enrolment and match loops
of do_enroll() and do_authenticate() (without Android's auth tokens),
and the database handling of fpc_set_active_group() and
fpc_tee_store_template_db(). The trustlet keeps its templates in a file
it reads and writes itself through two QSEE listener services, which the
driver serves from a thread the way the vendor's qseecomd does, confined
to /var/lib/fpctee.

The sensor has no bus libfprint can enumerate, so the device is
instantiated from the FPCTEE_DEVICE environment variable, naming the
node.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 libfprint/drivers/fpctee/fpctee.c | 1601 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 libfprint/meson.build             |    2 +
 meson.build                       |    2 +
 3 files changed, 1605 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 libfprint/drivers/fpctee/fpctee.c

diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/fpctee/fpctee.c b/libfprint/drivers/fpctee/fpctee.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e202b05
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpctee/fpctee.c
@@ -0,0 +1,1601 @@
+/*
+ * FPC match-on-chip fingerprint sensor behind a QSEE trustlet
+ *
+ * The sensor (an FPC1270 with an FPC2050 companion on the Google Pixel 2 XL)
+ * is owned by TrustZone. Enrolment, matching and template storage all happen
+ * inside the vendor's trustlet, fpctzappfingerprint; this driver drives it
+ * through the kernel's /dev/fpc_tee (drivers/misc/fpc1020.c), which carries
+ * command buffers to the trustlet, reports the sensor's interrupt level
+ * through poll(), and hands over the trustlet's file-service requests.
+ *
+ * The command protocol, its sequences and the meaning of its status codes
+ * are taken from the vendor's own HAL
+ * (android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.1-service.fpc, decompiled):
+ * fpc_tee_capture_image(), do_enroll(), do_authenticate(),
+ * fpc_set_active_group() and the fpc_tee_* command wrappers. The trustlet
+ * keeps its template database in a file it opens, sizes, reads and writes
+ * itself, through two QSEE listener services -- the POSIX-style file service
+ * (0xa) and the GP file service (0x7000). This driver serves both the way
+ * the vendor's qseecomd does (libdrmfs.so, dispatch() and gpdispatch()),
+ * confined to the database directory.
+ *
